<h2>The case in outline</h2>
<p>FIR No. 0613 of 2025, registered at P.S. Kotwali Jwalapur, Haridwar, alleged that the applicant maintained physical relations with the informant on a false promise of marriage. The original array of sections was broad — Sections 318(4), 351(2), 352, 64, 79 and 85 of the BNS, 2023. The investigation, however, culminated in a chargesheet limited to Sections 69, 351(2) and 352, and the case was pending as Criminal Case No. 4380 of 2026 before the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Haridwar. The applicant had enjoyed interim protection since December 2025 under an earlier writ petition, and had himself instituted a complaint case against the informant and others, in which process had been issued in June 2026.</p>
<p>Before the High Court, the defence described a consensual relationship between adults that began online, intertwined with monetary dealings, and an FIR lodged when demands for money were resisted. The State opposed, alleging that the applicant had prepared a forged affidavit describing himself as unmarried.</p>

<h2>The order</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>Weighing the rival submissions, the FIR and the applicant's own complaint case, the court concluded that the applicant deserved anticipatory bail, directing that upon arrest he be released forthwith on a personal bond with two reliable sureties of like amount to the satisfaction of the court concerned.</p>
</div>
<p>The order is brief, as anticipatory bail orders at this stage often are, and decides nothing about the forgery allegation or the truth of either narrative — those remain for the trial. Its significance lies in the configuration of factors that produced protection.</p>

<h2>Reading the decision in context</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>The chargesheet reframed the case.</strong> Once the investigating agency itself dropped Section 64 and proceeded under Section 69, the gravity calculus shifted: Section 69 carries imprisonment up to ten years, but it presupposes that the act does not amount to rape — a materially different accusation.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Custodial interrogation had no remaining role.</strong> With investigation complete and the chargesheet filed, arrest would serve process rather than discovery; courts are increasingly reluctant to insist on custody in such circumstances.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Cross-litigation told both ways.</strong> The applicant's own complaint case against the informant, instituted before the chargesheet, supported the defence theory of a monetary dispute — while remaining, like the FIR, an unproved allegation.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Protection had history.</strong> Interim protection had operated for nearly a year without reported misuse, itself evidence that liberty posed no investigative risk.</div>
</div>
<p>The decision sits alongside a growing body of BNS-era cases exploring Section 69 — a provision with no exact IPC predecessor, criminalising sexual intercourse procured by deceitful means or a false promise of marriage where the act is not rape. Courts have emphasised that the promise must be false at inception and causally linked to consent; relationships that sour, or founder on family opposition, are not automatically criminal. At the bail stage, the courts' task is narrower still: to decide whether arrest is necessary while those questions await trial.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Track the chargesheet closely: the dropping or addition of sections after investigation is often the single most important changed circumstance for a bail application.</p>
<p>Anticipatory bail remains available post-chargesheet if the applicant has not been arrested; frame the apprehension around process and warrants.</p>
<p>Where monetary dealings underlie the dispute, document them and, where genuine, pursue independent remedies; a contemporaneous complaint carries more weight than one improvised after the FIR.</p>
<p>Conduct during interim protection is evidence: appear when called, join the investigation, and keep the record of compliance.</p>
</div>
